The admin's look comes from `--mortar-*` CSS custom properties: colours, fonts, radii and gaps. A theme only sets those values. It never changes the layout.

## Using a theme package

Mortar ships four themes: `@mortar-kit/theme-midnight`, `-warm`, `-soft` and `-mono`. Pick one in `vite.config` with the `mortarKit()` plugin:

```ts
import { mortarKit } from '@mortar-kit/admin/vite';
import warm from '@mortar-kit/theme-warm';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [solid({ ... }), mortarKit({ theme: warm })],
});
```

The theme's stylesheet is served only with the admin's CSS, so it never lands on your public pages. This site's admin uses `midnight`.

## Your own values

The defaults sit at zero specificity, so any rule wins over them. Override a few on an element around the admin:

```css
.admin {
  --mortar-accent: #e11d48;
  --mortar-radius: 4px;
}
```

## Making a theme package

A theme package is plain JavaScript with no build: a `theme.css` that sets `--mortar-*` on `:root`, and an `index.js` that exports its name and the stylesheet's path:

```js
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';

export const name = 'forest';
export const stylesheet = fileURLToPath(new URL('./theme.css', import.meta.url));
export default { name, stylesheet };
```

The full list of tokens, with the default values, is in `packages/admin/src/tokens.css`. Fonts that a theme names aren't loaded by Mortar, so load them in your app or let the fallbacks apply.
